Tags: <a data-href="Network Security" href="tags/network-security.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Network Security</a> <a data-href="Threat Detection" href="tags/threat-detection.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Threat Detection</a> <a data-href="Log Management" href="tags/log-management.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Log Management</a> <a data-href="Regex" href="tags/regex.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Regex</a> <a data-href="SIEM" href="tags/siem.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">SIEM</a> <a data-href="DNS" href="tags/dns.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">DNS</a> <a data-href="Firewall" href="tags/firewall.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Firewall</a> <a data-href="Wazuh" href="tags/wazuh.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Wazuh</a> <a data-href="nftables" href="tags/nftables.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">nftables</a> <a data-href="dnsmasq" href="tags/dnsmasq.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">dnsmasq</a> <a data-href="Powershell" href="tags/powershell.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Powershell</a> <a data-href="Nmap" href="tags/nmap.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Nmap</a> <a data-href="Linux" href="tags/linux.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Linux</a><br><img alt="Pasted image 20260803175859.png" src="_files/pasted-image-20260803175859.png" target="_self">In my previous lab entries, I configured a network firewall and routed its logs to a Wazuh SIEM. However, raw logs have limited context, making it difficult for detection rules to correlate specific attributes such as DNS query type, source IP, and firewall action.To solve this, I created custom PCRE2 decoders to extract structured fields from nftables and dnsmasq logs. I then created detection rules mapped to the MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework to identify possible indicators of compromise, such as spikes in NXDOMAIN queries and abnormally long DNS queries. Identifying these attacks early can help defenders investigate and respond before they lead to further impact.<br>
<img alt="Pasted image 20260822161238.png" src="_files/pasted-image-20260822161238.png" target="_self">Network Topology Diagram
AntiX Firewall is running nftables, dnsmasq, and wireguard.<br>
<img alt="Pasted image 20260628125617.png" src="_files/pasted-image-20260628125617.png" target="_self"> Developed custom Wazuh decoders for dnsmasq and nftables telemetry using PCRE2 regular expressions. The parsers extract contextual fields such as source IP, DNS query type, requested domain, firewall action, and network interface. This enables more accurate rule matching and improves investigation and threat hunting.
Enhanced Nftables and Custom Dnsmasq Decoders Nftables
I first started by running wazuh-logtest and discovered nftables log matches with the built in kernel and its child iptables decoder which does not extract the fields that i needed so I created a custom decoder:
&lt;decoder name="kernel"&gt; &lt;program_name&gt;^kernel&lt;/program_name&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-nftables"&gt; &lt;parent&gt;kernel&lt;/parent&gt; &lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;NFT-(?:IN|FOR|OUT)-(?:DROP|ACCEPT)&lt;/prematch&gt; &lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;NFT-(IN|FOR|OUT)-(DROP|ACCEPT)(?:-(\S+))?\s+IN=(\S*)\s+OUT=(\S*)\s+(?:MAC=\S*\s+)?SRC=([0-9.]+)\s+DST=([0-9.]+).*?PROTO=(\w+)(?:\s+SPT=(\d+)\s+DPT=(\d+))?(?:\s+TYPE=(\d+)\s+CODE=(\d+))?&lt;/regex&gt;
order&gt;nft_chain,nft_action,nft_rule,nft_srcint,nft_dstint,srcip,dstip,protocol,srcport,dstport,icmp_type,icmp_code&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; File: /var/ossec/etc/decoders/nftables.xml (Wazuh_Server) Dnsmasq
While dnsmasq also matches a built-in Wazuh decoder, it was unable to extract any useful fields so i created a custom decoder for dnsmasq. There are multiple decoders this time because dnsmasq logs have multiple formats.
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq"&gt; &lt;program_name&gt;^dnsmasq&lt;/program_name&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; &lt;!-- log: dnsmasq: 1001 10.0.0.50/51234 query[A] ://google.com from 10.0.0.50 --&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq-query"&gt; &lt;parent&gt;antix-dnsmasq&lt;/parent&gt; &lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;query\[\w+\]&lt;/prematch&gt; &lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;^(\d+)\s+([0-9.]+)\/([0-9]+)\s+(query\[\w+\])\s+(\S+)&lt;/regex&gt; &lt;order&gt;id,srcip,srcport,dns_action,url&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; &lt;!-- log: dnsmasq: 1001 10.0.0.50/51234 forwarded ://google.com to 8.8.8.8 --&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq-forwarded"&gt;
&lt;parent&gt;antix-dnsmasq&lt;/parent&gt;
&lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;forwarded&lt;/prematch&gt;
&lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;^(\d+)\s+([0-9.]+)\/([0-9]+)\s+(forwarded)\s+(\S+)\s+to\s+(\S+)&lt;/regex&gt; &lt;order&gt;id,srcip,srcport,dns_action,url,resolved_to&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; &lt;!-- log: dnsmasq: 1001 10.0.0.50/51234 reply ://google.com is 142.250.190.36 --&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq-reply"&gt; &lt;parent&gt;antix-dnsmasq&lt;/parent&gt; &lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;reply&lt;/prematch&gt; &lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;^(\d+)\s+([0-9.]+)\/([0-9]+)\s+(reply)\s+(\S+)\s+is\s+(.+)&lt;/regex&gt; &lt;order&gt;id,srcip,srcport,dns_action,url,resolved_to&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; &lt;!-- log: dnsmasq: 1012 10.0.0.50/51245 config www.chess.com is NXDOMAIN --&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq-local"&gt;
&lt;parent&gt;antix-dnsmasq&lt;/parent&gt;
&lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;config|/etc/hosts|cached&lt;/prematch&gt;
&lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;^(\d+)\s+([0-9.]+)\/([0-9]+)\s+(\S+)\s+(\S+)\s+is\s+(.+)&lt;/regex&gt; &lt;order&gt;id,srcip,srcport,dns_action,url,resolved_to&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; &lt;!-- log: dnsmasq[6203]: exiting on receipt of SIGTERM --&gt;
&lt;decoder name="antix-dnsmasq-status"&gt; &lt;parent&gt;antix-dnsmasq&lt;/parent&gt; &lt;prematch type="pcre2"&gt;started|exiting&lt;/prematch&gt; &lt;regex type="pcre2"&gt;^(.+)&lt;/regex&gt; &lt;order&gt;status_msg&lt;/order&gt;
&lt;/decoder&gt; File: /var/ossec/etc/decoders/dnsmasq.xml (Wazuh_Server) Implemented DNS anomaly detection rules in Wazuh to identify characteristics commonly associated with DNS tunneling, such as unusually long query names and excessive TXT/NULL query activity.
Captures Some Possible DNS-based Attacks
Rule 100214 and 100215 will fire when the same IP performed a DNS query for long domain + txt/null and long domain + common record type respectively. These are possible indicators of exfiltration. Rule 100211 will trigger a level 4 alert when same source IP is requested for txt (typically used by attackers to transfer commands to the infected host) and null (rarely seen on production environment). Rule 100220 will trigger a level 7 alert when the same source IP performed 20 queries for non-existent domain within 60 second which can identify possible DGA attacks.
&lt;rule id="100214" level="5"&gt; &lt;if_sid&gt;100201&lt;/if_sid&gt; &lt;field name="dns_action" type="pcre2"&gt;query\[(TXT|NULL)\]&lt;/field&gt; &lt;url type="pcre2"&gt;^.{60,}$&lt;/url&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS query exceeded expected domain length threshold using uncommon record type.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt; &lt;rule id="100215" level="3"&gt; &lt;if_sid&gt;100201&lt;/if_sid&gt; &lt;field name="dns_action" type="pcre2"&gt;query\[(A|AAAA|CNAME|SRV|MX|PTR)\]&lt;/field&gt; &lt;url type="pcre2"&gt;^.{60,}$&lt;/url&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS query exceeded expected domain length threshold using common record type.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt;
&lt;rule id="100211" level="4"&gt; &lt;if_sid&gt;100201&lt;/if_sid&gt; &lt;field name="dns_action" type="pcre2"&gt;query\[(TXT|NULL)\]&lt;/field&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS query observed for uncommon resource record type.&lt;/description&gt; &lt;mitre&gt; &lt;id&gt;T1071.004&lt;/id&gt; &lt;/mitre&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt;
&lt;rule id="100207" level="2"&gt; &lt;field name="resolved_to"&gt;NXDOMAIN&lt;/field&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS response returned NXDOMAIN status.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt;
&lt;rule id="100220" level="7" frequency="20" timeframe="60"&gt; &lt;if_matched_sid&gt;100207&lt;/if_matched_sid&gt; &lt;same_srcip /&gt; &lt;description&gt;Repeated NXDOMAIN responses observed from same source IP. Possible DGA-like domain activity or reconnaissance behavior.&lt;/description&gt; &lt;mitre&gt; &lt;id&gt;T1568.002&lt;/id&gt; &lt;/mitre&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt; File: /var/ossec/etc/rules/100200-dnsmasq.xml (Wazuh_Server) Implemented behavioral DNS detection by monitoring abnormal NXDOMAIN response frequency from individual hosts. This helps identify potential DGA activity where a malware is looking for the command-and-control server.
Detecting Possible DGA Attack
The nature of DGA (Domain Generation Algorithm) attacks makes it extremely difficult for hardcoded firewall signatures to detect and completely block them. So I implemented a behavioral ruleset that monitors and generates a high-severity alert when it detected a host querying 20 non-existent domains within 60 seconds which could be an indicator of DGA activity.
&lt;rule id="100203" level="2"&gt; &lt;if_sid&gt;100200&lt;/if_sid&gt; &lt;field name="dns_action"&gt;reply&lt;/field&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS response received from upstream resolver.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt; &lt;rule id="100207" level="2"&gt; &lt;if_sid&gt;100203&lt;/if_sid&gt; &lt;field name="resolved_to"&gt;NXDOMAIN&lt;/field&gt; &lt;description&gt;DNS response returned NXDOMAIN status.&lt;/description&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt; &lt;rule id="100220" level="7" frequency="20" timeframe="60"&gt; &lt;if_matched_sid&gt;100207&lt;/if_matched_sid&gt; &lt;same_srcip /&gt; &lt;description&gt;Repeated NXDOMAIN responses observed from same source IP. Possible DGA-like domain activity or reconnaissance behavior.&lt;/description&gt; &lt;mitre&gt; &lt;id&gt;T1568.002&lt;/id&gt; &lt;/mitre&gt;
&lt;/rule&gt; Designed correlation-based detection rules using frequency and timeframe thresholds to reduce low-confidence alerts. Informational events are retained for visibility, while higher severity alerts require repeated suspicious behavior patterns.
<br>Frequency-based Rule Design While every log is decoded, many are retained solely for backend visibility and correlation. Base rules will rarely trigger on a single log, instead, it must meet the specified frequency thresholds or trigger a chain of rules in a short period of time. <img alt="Pasted image 20260822233457.png" src="_files/pasted-image-20260822233457.png" target="_self">
