Attack Execution
To emulate a basic DGA attack traffic, I used a PowerShell script that uses a simple date-based computation as a seed for a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG). The script then generates and queries 30 pseudo-random {domain}.com names, causing the host to receive a high number of NXDOMAIN responses. Although the DGA script specifies a custom DNS resolver, the firewall redirects DNS traffic to dnsmasq, allowing the queries to be logged and analyzed by Wazuh.
$TargetDNS = "8.8.8.8"
# 1. Gather date variables
$Date = Get-Date
$Year = $Date.Year
$Day = $Date.Day
$DayOfWeek = $Date.DayOfWeek.ToString()
$CountDayOfWeek = $DayOfWeek.Length # 2. Calculate the Custom Key: (Year * Day * count(dayofweek)) - Day $Key = ($Year * $Day * $CountDayOfWeek) - $Day # 3. Seed the Random Number Generator for reproducible DGA output
$Rand = New-Object System.Random($Key) # 4. Generate 30 domains and blast the DNS server
for ($i = 1; $i -le 30; $i++) { # Generate a random domain length between 10 and 15 characters $DomainLength = $Rand.Next(10, 16) # Pick random lowercase letters (ASCII 97-122) $Chars = (1..$DomainLength) | ForEach-Object { [char]$Rand.Next(97, 123) } $Domain = (-join $Chars) + ".com" # Send the query and ignore the inevitable NXDOMAIN errors Resolve-DnsName -Name $Domain -Server $TargetDNS -QuickTimeout -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null # Tiny sleep to ensure packets space out properly Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 50
} File: dga_v1.ps1 Aggregated Dnsmasq Logs on Wazuh
